      "writtenOn": "2025-06-23T23:10:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "A point raised by Greg offline: from security standpoint, perhaps it\u0027s better to not have this code in the hypervisor, deliberately making life easier for the attacker (in case there is a vulnerability in pKVM already being exploited, all the attacker needs to do to gain full control over the system is to jump to the reprivileging code)?\n\nSo perhaps it\u0027s better keep it simple and just panic, or for example just taint kernel, if pKVM init failed during or after pkvm_host_deprivilege_cpus()?",
      "revId": "948704b1f794fbc32de454613cdcd2ae4bf3b2b4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "eed2bc6a_32e191b4",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 11,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-24T10:47:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I agree, if pkvm is compromised, there is a risk of attacker to reprivilege the host. But if pkvm is compromised, then all bets are already off isn\u0027t it? I also agree with not continuing the boot, but tainting the kernel and booting with p partially deprivileged cpus doesn\u0027t appeal to me much. I could also go a bit further to zero out the reprivilege code once the pkvm has been fully initialized so that attacker would not be able to use the logic any more if pkvm is compromised. What do you think?",
      "parentUuid": "405ab7f5_adc313d5",
      "revId": "948704b1f794fbc32de454613cdcd2ae4bf3b2b4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
